00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.789 align:start position:0%
on april 30th people here in sweden and  in neighboring countries like germany  finland denmark and estonia celebrate an  annual feast day known locally as vol  boris mesoafton or more often called by  the shorter name vol bori it is a day  that is celebrated in different ways  across different regions but it's  generally a communal event where people  gather to light big bonfires sing songs  and hold speeches to announce the  arrival of summer and for students at  various universities as is usually the  case a chance to get very very drunk  but what are the origins of this feast  why do we celebrate it and what's up  with the bonfires  volbois messofton translates as the eve  of walpurgis's mass and is often called  simply alpergus knight in english the  tradition dates back to medieval times  in germany from where it's spread to  neighboring countries including here in  sweden as is often the case with  traditions here it originates in  catholicism and with an eighth century  english missionary called saint walpurga  while program from which the swedish  name vol bali derives was a prominent  christian figure at the time who came to  germany as a missionary with the aim of  converting the local population to  christianity she appears to have been  quite successful at this and pretty  shortly after her death around the year  777 she was canonized as a saint on may  the first 870. and this is actually the  origin of this feast day it is a  celebration of the canonization of saint  valporga on may the 1st the celebration  on april 30th called the eve of saint  paul progress's mass is as the name  suggests a celebration of the eve before  may 1st much like christmas eve or  easter eve as it is known over here and  since the middle ages this day has been  celebrated across northern europe it has  direct origins in religious history and  in the catholic church but walborg is  also a lot more than this  it just so happens to fall on an  important non-religious day as well the  last day of april has been considered a  transitionary point where winter turns  to spring and summer  it is often on may 1st that the cows and  other cattle are released and allowed to  graze outside again for the first time  during the year  in sweden and in the general  consciousness of most regular people who  partake in the celebrations this  connection to the arrival of spring is a  lot more apparent and important than a  relatively obscure christian saint that  most people don't know about anyway  volboy is quite a popular feast day that  many people participate in in different  ways there are some recurring features  of this day which everyone knows about  and does but as is often the case when  it comes to religious rituals and  traditions people don't always really  know why they are doing any of those  rituals on well perguso's night the  swedes will gather outside to socialize  usually the local government or  inhabitants have arranged activities  like food musical performances and  generally to promote a feeling of  community  music plays an important role as choirs  will often perform traditional songs in  celebration of the arrival of spring  it's also common that a local celebrity  or politician holds a speech on the same  subject volvo has become an especially  important holiday for the students in  the country barbara celebrations at  various universities like that in  uppsala can be very intricate and  complex involving various steps and  stages like marches  choir singing picnics champagne  breakfasts and in general the  consumption of large amounts of alcohol  celebrations usually take place in a  relatively open field and the most  important and common feature of volboy  is the lighting of a big  bonfire  the origins and reasons behind this  bonfire isn't entirely known and various  theories are often put forth saint  valperga herself became strongly  associated with protection against evil  spirits and witches some traditions  claim that on april 30th because of this  transition between winter and spring and  summer witches and evil spirits are more  active and the most common explanation  for the bonfire is that it has the  purpose of warding away these malignant  forces others argue that it is related  to the arrival of spring and the  releasing of cattle to outside  environments as a result of this the  farmers would light bonfires to ward off  predators that could easily prey on the  newly released cattle in any case the  reason behind the fires and all of the  other rituals aren't really that  important this is a good example to show  the nature of how religious traditions  and rituals generally work people don't  always care why they do things but only  care about their very doing itself  most swedes don't light the bonfire and  celebrate volboy because they want to  remember saint walpurga and her powers  to ward off evil forces they do it  because everyone does it they do it  because their parents did it because  it's just something we do  religion and rituals is first and  foremost something that we do as many  scholars of religion today argue the  action or performing of a ritual itself  is what always comes first it's the  priority any rationalization about why a  ritual was performed comes secondarily  if at all it is through ritual actions  and ritualization that we create  meanings that we create social bonds and  identities and that is at least  according to one interpretation what  religion and ritual is all about and any  sort of metaphysical or cosmological  meaning behind any ritual in that sense  is really  secondary rituals like that on  walborough's mesoafton are performed not  because of some underlying theological  or existential purpose or reason but  because they bring a sense of  familiarity and comfort it creates a  sense of social belonging and cohesion  being a communal festivity in which a  large amount of people participate it's  not about saint walpurga or really about  the arrival of spring and summer but  about us and how we interact and create  bonds and connections to other people  and a larger social context  and i think all this can give us a few  things to think about when it comes to  traditions what their functions are and  how we relate to them  gladwaldboy and i'll see you next time  [Music]  foreign
 

